UnitTests provide an easy way to perform automated testing (including)
regression-testing and are desbribed in depth at
http://c2.com/cgi/wiki?UnitTest

The UnitTest framework MuleUnit is a minimalistic UnitTesting-framework
based on the EasyUnit framework, with the goal of making testing of the
aMule codebase easier.


How to use:
 This section describes the step-by-step of creating a new testcase.

 It is recommended that each test-case (a collection of tests) is
 placed in a separate .cpp file, in general, one test-case per class.

 In the following examples, I will test a non-existing stack template
 class which has the member-functions push (back) and pop (front).


 1) Creating the test skeleton:
    Test-cases exist in two flavors: With a fixture and without.
    A fixture is a way to ease the preparation before and after
    each test and consists of (optionally) a setUp function which
    is run before each unittest, a tearDown function which is run
    after each unittest and any number of member-variables and
    helper-functions.


    A simple testcase (in this case for the Stack class) is declared
    like this, though the name "StackTest" may be anything.

      #include <muleunit/test.h>

      DECLARE_SIMPLE(StackTest);


    If we wanted to have a default stack-object created before each test
    and deleted afterwards, we could use a fixture, which is done like so:

      #include <muleunit/test.h>

      DECLARE(StackTest);
        Stack<int>* m_stack;

        //! Called before each test.
        void setUp() {
            m_stack = new Stack<int>();
        }

        //! Called after each test
        void tearDown() {
            delete m_stack;
        }
      END_DECLARE();


    Both DECLARE statements creates a base-class from which the unittests
    are derived, and the section between DECLARE(..) and END_DECLARE() are
    used verbatim in the definition of said baseclass.


 2) Writing Tests
    To add an actual test to the test-case, the following is done:

      TEST(StackTest, AStackTest)
      {
        <test here>
      }


    This will create and register a unittest to the StackTest
    test-case which has the name "AStackTest". An simple example
    of this can be seen here:

      TEST(StackTest, PushAndPop)
      {
        m_stack->push(10);

        ASSERT_EQUALS(10, m_stack->pop());
      }


    A test-case can have any number of unittests associated with it,
    but each test in a test-case must be uniquely named.


  3) Building the test
    Assuming that the testcase described above has been placed in
    tests/StackTest.cpp, add a target for it to tests/CMakeLists.txt:

      add_executable (StackTest StackTest.cpp)
      add_test (NAME StackTest COMMAND StackTest)
      target_include_directories (StackTest PRIVATE
      	${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}
      	${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/src
      	${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/src/include
      )
      target_link_libraries (StackTest muleunit mulecommon)

    Link mulecommon even when the test seems not to need it: some
    linkers (GNU ld, MinGW) need it to resolve muleunit. Add the source
    files of the code under test to add_executable when they are not
    part of a library.

    The tests are built only when BUILD_TESTING is on:

      $ cmake -B build -DBUILD_TESTING=YES <your options>
      $ cmake --build build


  4) Running the test
    Run ctest from the build directory:

      $ cd build
      $ ctest --output-on-failure

    To run one test-case, name it with -R, or run its binary, which
    prints the result of each test:

      $ ctest -R StackTest
      $ ./unittests/tests/StackTest

      Running test-collection "StackTest" with 1 test-cases:
      	Test "AStackTest"

    Should a test fail, the binary prints where, and exits with a
    non-zero status:

      	Test "Foo"
      		Failure running:
      		 StackTest.cpp:11 -- Test failed.


  5) More
    More information about available test-macros can be found in the
    muleunit/test.h header-file.


  6) The curl suites
    curl-tests/ holds shell tests for the HTTP interfaces. They are not
    part of ctest, because they need a running daemon.

    curl-tests/amuleapi/run-all.sh tests amuleapi. It needs an amuled
    with External Connections on, and it starts a fresh amuleapi for
    each script. By default it connects to EC on 127.0.0.1:4712 with
    the password "amule" (set EC_HOST, EC_PORT and EC_PASSWORD to
    change this), and uses the newest build*/src/webapi/amuleapi:

      $ cd unittests/curl-tests/amuleapi
      $ ./run-all.sh                          # every script
      $ ./run-all.sh 12-downloads-add-patch.sh  # some scripts only

    Some scripts need a live ed2k or Kad connection. The CI job in
    .github/workflows/ccpp.yml shows how to start an isolated amuled,
    and which scripts it skips and why.

    The scripts share one harness, curl-tests/amuleapi/lib/common.sh:
    counters, _curl, the _assert_* helpers, _login and _summary. A new
    script sources it, calls _preflight and ends with _summary. Keep
    the lib/ directory next to a script that is copied elsewhere.

    curl-tests/amuleweb-smoke/phase0.sh checks the landing pages of a
    running amuleweb. See the header of the script for its settings.

AllSearch integration coverage (Linux, Python 3, BUILD_DAEMON=ON):

  ctest --test-dir build -R 'AllSearch|SearchSourceCount|SearchRequest|SearchProgressReport|SearchHistory' --output-on-failure

AllSearchIntegrationTest starts an isolated daemon with temporary HOME/config
and a loopback eD2k server. Bootstrap downloads are disabled and Kad has no
public contacts. It checks unavailable-network fallback, query encoding,
both component completion orders, duplicate keywords, stop/close, and restored
search types after restart. Rejected replacements and independent Kad starts must
preserve a pending eD2k search. It also exercises duplicate/grouped results,
source-count aggregation and persistence, repeated close/restart, late server
replies after close, bulk Kad stop, and shutdown with active searches.
Truncated saved-search fixtures exercise cleanup after a complete
root result and after a complete child result. Every daemon shutdown must exit
successfully, so sanitizer failures fail the test. It does not use an existing
daemon or user profile.

With BUILD_AMULEAPI=ON and BUILD_AMULECMD=ON, AllSearchClientCapabilityTest
also checks a loopback EC peer omitting EC_TAG_CAN_SEARCH_ALL: the API returns
503 ec_unsupported and amulecmd reports a clear error; neither sends SEARCH_START.
The offline 45-all-search.sh curl phase covers REST/SSE discovery, API starts,
Kad-only fallback, component activity, More, stop and close. When amulecmd is
built alongside amuled, it also starts an All search with command-line filters.

For a sanitizer-instrumented daemon, run this script directly with
ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1:halt_on_error=1 and
UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:print_stacktrace=1:

  python3 unittests/tests/AllSearchIntegrationTest.py <path-to-amuled>

The existing wxGetApp downcast during CamuleApp construction triggers the UBSan
vptr check before startup. If encountered, a temporary suppression file containing
"vptr:wxGetApp" can be passed via UBSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=<file> alongside the
options above; keep address and leak checking enabled.

Runtime network preference coverage (Linux, BUILD_DAEMON=ON, BUILD_AMULEAPI=ON,
ENABLE_INTEGRATION_TESTS=ON):

  ctest --test-dir build -R NetworkPreferencesIntegrationTest --output-on-failure

NetworkPreferencesIntegrationTest starts an isolated offline daemon with both
networks disabled and seeded server.met/staticservers.dat files. A REST PATCH
must enable ED2K without restarting, load both lists, create the server UDP
socket and attempt the configured auto-update once. Repeated network toggles
must preserve the live list. A second EC client checks all enabled-network
bitmasks through the connection-state poll, including partial preference writes
and both networks disabled. It also stops a running, unconnected Kad and a
loopback ED2K connection and a pending server login (including TCP socket closure),
and checks that a disabled shutdown saves an initialized server list while a
disabled restart preserves an uninitialized list. The
headless ECPrefsDiffTest covers reverse toggles after a remote poll, preserving
unrelated edits, and merging open-dialog checkboxes without losing staged edits.
The auto-update has an empty URL list; no public
network connection is required.

GUI follow-up: with amulegui connected (or EC enabled on the monolithic GUI),
toggle ED2K and Kad from the Web UI. Check that the Networks page, conditional
log tabs and toolbar label follow each change within one poll. In amulegui,
then change each network back in Preferences and verify that the daemon accepts
it. Reopen Preferences/reconnect to check the full preference refresh path.

With Preferences open on Connection, stage an ED2K checkbox change and change
Kad through another client. The staged ED2K choice must remain, while the
unedited Kad checkbox follows the external change. Save and verify both flags.
While a network edit is staged, change the same flag to the staged value and back
from another client. The staged checkbox must survive both changes. Cancel and
reopen Preferences to verify it follows the current core value again.
Repeat without a staged edit: both checkboxes, the Servers sidebar entry and
the server UDP label must follow external changes. Finally stage a network
change and enable API guest access without a password: the rejected save must
leave the core network flags unchanged. Supply valid settings and retry; the
staged network change must then apply. Run these checks in both desktop clients.

Upload storage-delay regression (Linux)
--------------------------------------
With BUILD_DAEMON=ON, BUILD_TESTING=ON and ENABLE_INTEGRATION_TESTS=ON:

  cmake --build build --target amuled UploadReadDelay
  ctest --test-dir build -R '^UploadRefillIntegrationTest$' --output-on-failure

UploadRefillIntegrationTest also runs the controlled storage-delay cases, using
the existing CI integration-test budget. The delay cases can be run separately:

  python3 unittests/tests/UploadStorageDelayIntegrationTest.py \
    build/src/amuled build/unittests/tests/libUploadReadDelay.so

The delay test uses isolated offline daemon profiles and two loopback peers. A
test-only LD_PRELOAD library gates actual reads of one synthetic shared file with pipes.
While a later read is held, the preceding block must already reach its peer.
Each client keeps at most one read outstanding: the other peer must be served
before the busy peer's twelfth read, for small, full, three-block and mixed
request sizes.
Payload contents and packet continuity are checked throughout. No production
hooks, public peers, storage hardware or timed sleeps simulate the delay.
These cases test delivery and sweep fairness. Progress on other storage while
an individual read is blocked is covered by the pool cases below.

Upload storage-worker isolation (Linux)
--------------------------------------
UploadRefillIntegrationTest also runs UploadStoragePoolIntegrationTest.py using
that same preload fixture and CI integration budget. To run just the pool cases:

  python3 unittests/tests/UploadStoragePoolIntegrationTest.py \
    build/src/amuled build/unittests/tests/libUploadReadDelay.so

The fixture substitutes only the process's mount-table input, then holds actual
reads or opens behind pipe gates. Four clients request files from one storage
ID through two directory aliases; another storage ID must continue uploading
before any gate is released. The test checks the per-storage limit, recovery,
a disconnect while blocked, same-client/same-block upload-session reuse, and
shutdown while a worker still owns a read. A compression gate also reuses a
client/socket while its old session builds packets outside the upload-list lock;
only the new session may receive data. Additional cases verify that heap reads
reuse their original buffer and that mapped reads copy successfully or reject a
SIGBUS caused by truncating the file after prefaulting, without ending the daemon. It also checks that two slow-volume
peers alternate while a fast-volume peer completes a read between every release,
and that uploads continue while storage discovery is blocked. No system mounts
are changed.
StorageReadPoolTest covers admission, retained completion budgets and worker
exceptions without a daemon. UploadStorageMapTest checks prefix boundaries,
lexical path normalization, mount escapes, macOS firmlink spellings and Windows
mapped-drive/UNC aliases. A pool unit test checks that a refreshed storage key
continues to reserve the permit for an already outstanding read.

The pool has four outstanding ownership slots, at most one per filesystem/volume
identity, including completed results not yet delivered. File open/read/close
operations stay on workers; packet delivery uses copied buffers. Complete-file
handles remain cached (32 entries); uncached and idle-handle closes use workers
and the same storage limits. One pending read per client and admission in order
of oldest successful dispatch keep peers on the same volume fair, independently
of how frequently other volumes complete reads.

The coordinator queues all currently ready completions before waking the upload
throttler once for that batch. An empty batch, discarded session or close-only
completion does not trigger a wake. Draining never waits for outstanding reads,
so a stalled volume cannot hold back packets from a completed read.

Linux identifies groups from mountinfo device IDs, macOS/FreeBSD from cached
mount IDs, and Windows from volume GUIDs (UNC paths use the server/share).
On macOS, the root fallback shares the Data volume's key when that mount exists,
so firmlink and explicit Data spellings cannot bypass the volume limit. Windows
mapped drives use WNetGetConnectionW to share their UNC server/share key.
Discovery runs on one bounded pool slot; lookups use the last successful snapshot
while a provider is blocked. Windows publishes a local-only snapshot before
consulting network providers at startup. Refreshing reclassifies outstanding
permits and cached handles before admitting new reads. Unmapped paths, including
reads before the first successful snapshot, share one conservative group.
These are filesystem/volume limits,
not physical-controller topology: different volumes on the same hardware can
consume separate slots. Paths crossing volumes through symlinks may also be
grouped conservatively by their spelled mount path. Four distinct stalled groups
can occupy all four workers. Shutdown joins accepted reads; it cannot interrupt
an indefinitely blocked kernel call or safely detach a worker using core data.

Native storage-worker acceptance (Windows, macOS and FreeBSD)
-----------------------------------------------------------
The Linux fixture and cross-platform builds do not validate native discovery.
Use an isolated offline profile and disposable shared files, with loopback peers
on two existing volumes. Record the source revision, OS version, build type,
toolchain, filesystem types and whether the paths are local or network shares.

First verify byte-correct transfers from both volumes. Then delay reads on one
volume with a platform-native test fixture and verify that peers on the other
volume keep receiving data before the delay is released. Request from several
paths on the delayed volume, including a second mount path/drive letter for that
same volume where available: they must share one outstanding job. On Windows,
also check that two directories on one UNC server/share are grouped together
with mapped-drive spellings and that a separate share can progress. On macOS,
compare /private/tmp paths with /System/Volumes/Data/private/tmp spellings.
Queue two peers on the slow volume alongside a fast-volume peer; both slow peers
must make progress. Do not create aliases or change system mounts merely to run
this check.

Release the delay and verify recovery and payload integrity. Repeat with a
disconnect and upload-slot reuse while the read is held; an old completion must
not reach the new session. Finally request shutdown while a read is held: the
application must wait for that read, then exit cleanly when it is released.
Keep opens, reads and closes off the coordinator in each native run. Record
unavailable alias/UNC/fault-injection cases as untested, rather than treating a
successful build or ordinary two-volume transfer as an isolation test.

EC integration-test requirements
--------------------------------
Tests that authenticate through ECWire require Python 3 with the cryptography
module (python3-cryptography on Debian/Ubuntu). These tests return skip code 77
when the module is unavailable, including fixtures importing the EC helper.
